      "bodyTr": "The \"manipulated indictment special prosecutor\" bill passed through the National Assembly's Legislation and Judiciary Committee yesterday under the leadership of the Democratic Party. The People Power Party opposed it, calling it a \"bill to erase President Lee Jae-myung's crimes,\" but had little choice but to walk out of the chamber. Following the party line set at a general meeting of lawmakers the day before, the Democratic Party plans to present a revised bill to the plenary session today, with some clauses including the special prosecutor's authority to withdraw indictments deleted. The intent is to dispel suspicions of a \"special prosecutor for presidential indictment withdrawal,\" but most citizens are aware that this bill is a step toward granting immunity to the current president. There are also arguments that deleting these clauses is mere window dressing, since indictment withdrawal becomes possible when special prosecutor investigation results showing manipulation are transferred to the prosecutor's office. Isn't this type of special prosecutor precisely the model of political prosecution that the Democratic Party once despised?\n\nThe official title of the special prosecutor bill itself foreshadows its investigation results: \"Bill on the Appointment of Special Prosecutors to Investigate the Truth Regarding Suspicions of Manipulated Investigations and Indictments by the Yoon Suk-yeol Administration's Prosecution, National Intelligence Service, Board of Audit, and Others.\" Given this, what investigation results can the special prosecutor appointed under these circumstances possibly deliver besides \"manipulation\"? This is precisely why a current prosecutor at the prosecutor's office appealed against the injustice, writing that having the ruling party take the lead in creating a special prosecutor to reinvestigate the president's own criminal case, and having the president appoint the special prosecutor, constitutes \"privatization of power\" and clear \"conflict of interest.\" Even citizens unfamiliar with the law have unresolved doubts. It is only natural to wonder: \"Why does the president refuse to go through the same trial procedures that all citizens undergo and instead seek indictment withdrawal through procedures designed solely for him?\" Moreover, prosecutors dispatched to the special prosecutor's team will be granted investigative authority. While the state has completely stripped prosecutors of investigative authority in cases where citizens are victims, the state now grants prosecutors investigative authority for investigations benefiting the president. This is a contradiction. The Justice Ministry's fact-finding team is already investigating cases related to the current president, making it difficult to accept the duplication of personnel and budget. This accumulated sense of unease is influencing negative assessments of the current president and the Democratic Party. When the special prosecutor begins operations, the Democratic Party may face a crisis more serious than the ills of past political prosecution.",
